1
00:00:00,672 --> 00:00:02,323
Now, when it comes to tweaking those

2
00:00:02,355 --> 00:00:04,598
settings, you can of course also remember

3
00:00:04,598 --> 00:00:06,680
them so that they will be used in the future

4
00:00:06,680 --> 00:00:09,468
again. So if you by default want, let's say,

5
00:00:09,484 --> 00:00:12,063
10,000 tokens, you could save these settings

6
00:00:12,063 --> 00:00:14,050
as a default so that you don't always have

7
00:00:14,050 --> 00:00:16,101
to change it again when you reload the

8
00:00:16,101 --> 00:00:16,421
model.

9
00:00:17,815 --> 00:00:19,946
But you also have some advanced settings

10
00:00:19,962 --> 00:00:22,189
here. Now, to be very honest, most of these

11
00:00:22,221 --> 00:00:24,384
settings can stay the way they are. The

12
00:00:24,384 --> 00:00:26,514
evaluation batch size is an interesting one

13
00:00:26,627 --> 00:00:28,613
because here you can control the number of

14
00:00:28,693 --> 00:00:31,386
input tokens that are processed at a time,

15
00:00:31,450 --> 00:00:33,696
so simultaneously by the model.

16
00:00:34,466 --> 00:00:36,391
And you could increase this to improve the

17
00:00:36,391 --> 00:00:38,910
performance of the model at the cost of

18
00:00:39,038 --> 00:00:40,065
using more memory.

19
00:00:40,739 --> 00:00:42,391
But I typically keep the default here as

20
00:00:42,504 --> 00:00:43,996
well. But you could experiment with

21
00:00:44,188 --> 00:00:45,167
different values here.

22
00:00:45,953 --> 00:00:48,151
The other settings, to be very honest, are

23
00:00:48,215 --> 00:00:51,006
settings I never touch because the defaults

24
00:00:51,006 --> 00:00:51,680
work quite well.

25
00:00:52,370 --> 00:00:55,418
These ropey settings are about pushing the

26
00:00:55,418 --> 00:00:58,257
boundaries of your model when it comes to

27
00:00:58,450 --> 00:01:01,081
operating with lots of context. So long

28
00:01:01,193 --> 00:01:04,677
context documents or chat histories. And I

29
00:01:04,805 --> 00:01:07,823
typically prefer to split complex long

30
00:01:07,935 --> 00:01:10,680
context tasks into simpler, shorter

31
00:01:10,680 --> 00:01:13,104
subtasks, because in my experience, that

32
00:01:13,217 --> 00:01:15,528
yields better results than operating on

33
00:01:15,721 --> 00:01:17,615
super long context anyways.

34
00:01:18,530 --> 00:01:20,568
Now, what is interesting are the flash

35
00:01:20,633 --> 00:01:23,683
attention and the key and V-cache settings

36
00:01:23,747 --> 00:01:24,630
down here though.

37
00:01:25,577 --> 00:01:28,049
And the idea behind all these settings

38
00:01:28,402 --> 00:01:29,797
especially when used together,

39
00:01:30,390 --> 00:01:33,661
is that they can, as the tooltip tells us,

40
00:01:34,399 --> 00:01:37,622
decrease the memory usage and also the

41
00:01:37,622 --> 00:01:40,685
generation time of some models depending on

42
00:01:40,733 --> 00:01:41,920
how well they support it.

43
00:01:42,834 --> 00:01:45,464
Now, this quantization, this KV cache

44
00:01:45,464 --> 00:01:48,494
quantization, is not the quantization we

45
00:01:48,526 --> 00:01:50,707
were referring to earlier in the course when

46
00:01:50,755 --> 00:01:52,664
we explored hardware requirements.

47
00:01:53,337 --> 00:01:56,496
Instead, flash attention and KV cache

48
00:01:56,608 --> 00:01:58,917
quantization, these 3 settings in the end

49
00:01:58,949 --> 00:01:59,639
work together

50
00:02:00,377 --> 00:02:02,801
As you also see here, flash attention must

51
00:02:02,801 --> 00:02:05,674
be enabled for V-cache quantization at

52
00:02:05,674 --> 00:02:08,307
least. And the idea behind these settings is

53
00:02:09,142 --> 00:02:13,556
that the model has to process less tokens to

54
00:02:13,556 --> 00:02:16,253
generate a new output token, because

55
00:02:16,301 --> 00:02:16,783
normally

56
00:02:17,344 --> 00:02:19,560
it always has to process all existing

57
00:02:19,560 --> 00:02:22,642
tokens, including existing output tokens it

58
00:02:22,706 --> 00:02:25,820
generated, to produce the next token in

59
00:02:25,836 --> 00:02:27,698
line, which makes sense because that next

60
00:02:27,762 --> 00:02:31,150
token depends on all these other tokens. Now

61
00:02:31,375 --> 00:02:34,203
with flash attention and KV cache

62
00:02:34,814 --> 00:02:36,244
quantization enabled,

63
00:02:36,838 --> 00:02:39,763
roughly speaking, tokens generated in

64
00:02:40,036 --> 00:02:43,828
earlier steps can be cached and therefore be

65
00:02:43,989 --> 00:02:46,961
remembered and can therefore be reused in

66
00:02:46,961 --> 00:02:50,512
subsequent steps to generate future tokens

67
00:02:51,042 --> 00:02:54,818
without having to recalculate them because

68
00:02:55,381 --> 00:02:58,192
without this turned on, the model

69
00:02:58,192 --> 00:03:01,437
essentially recalculates all previous tokens

70
00:03:01,886 --> 00:03:03,557
just to derive that next token.

71
00:03:04,392 --> 00:03:06,850
With flash attention and disk caching

72
00:03:06,898 --> 00:03:09,853
enabled, it can simply load

73
00:03:10,721 --> 00:03:13,066
those old calculations from memory and

74
00:03:13,066 --> 00:03:15,523
therefore derive the next token quicker.

75
00:03:16,439 --> 00:03:18,222
Now, at the point of time where I'm

76
00:03:18,270 --> 00:03:20,181
recording this, this is an experimental

77
00:03:20,197 --> 00:03:23,185
feature. And as this message here tells us,

78
00:03:23,265 --> 00:03:24,759
it may cause issues with

79
00:03:25,514 --> 00:03:27,376
because not all models are

80
00:03:27,922 --> 00:03:30,667
great at using this mechanism or are aware

81
00:03:30,795 --> 00:03:31,790
of this technique.

82
00:03:32,722 --> 00:03:35,579
You can nonetheless consider or try turning

83
00:03:35,627 --> 00:03:38,501
this on and simply experiment with the model

84
00:03:38,581 --> 00:03:41,310
with that being turned on to see whether it

85
00:03:41,310 --> 00:03:43,381
makes a difference and whether it works well

86
00:03:43,477 --> 00:03:43,798
for you.

87
00:03:45,259 --> 00:03:47,153
For example, here with that turned on,

88
00:03:47,731 --> 00:03:49,449
if I now ask it to

89
00:03:50,396 --> 00:03:51,552
write an essay

90
00:03:52,607 --> 00:03:54,015
about a rabbit,

91
00:03:55,098 --> 00:03:56,878
it's quite quick at doing so.

92
00:03:57,687 --> 00:04:00,388
And the text also doesn't look too bad to

93
00:04:00,388 --> 00:04:00,631
me.

94
00:04:03,198 --> 00:04:05,820
And it generated those 1,000 tokens

95
00:04:06,484 --> 00:04:10,515
at a rate of around 31.3 tokens per second.

96
00:04:12,067 --> 00:04:14,711
Now, if I reload this model, but I turn off

97
00:04:14,760 --> 00:04:16,587
flash attention and KV cache,

98
00:04:18,269 --> 00:04:19,728
and I use that same prompt

99
00:04:20,685 --> 00:04:22,080
in a new chat thereafter,

100
00:04:24,539 --> 00:04:26,651
it of course again goes ahead and generates

101
00:04:26,651 --> 00:04:27,063
a text.

102
00:04:30,051 --> 00:04:33,169
And for me here, it generated 1,000 tokens

103
00:04:33,347 --> 00:04:36,174
at a rate of only 23 tokens per second. So

104
00:04:36,174 --> 00:04:39,112
quite a bit slower than with flash attention

105
00:04:39,112 --> 00:04:39,546
turned on.

106
00:04:40,786 --> 00:04:43,153
And that's precisely the idea behind flash

107
00:04:43,265 --> 00:04:44,698
attention and KV caching.

108
00:04:45,904 --> 00:04:48,682
The idea is to give you faster generations,

109
00:04:48,939 --> 00:04:51,074
but you should carefully check whether the

110
00:04:51,122 --> 00:04:54,350
results are good or if your model is having

111
00:04:54,398 --> 00:04:56,533
any problems with these settings. In that

112
00:04:56,581 --> 00:04:58,396
case, you should of course turn off these

113
00:04:58,604 --> 00:04:59,038
settings.

114
00:04:59,953 --> 00:05:01,767
But from all these more advanced settings,

115
00:05:02,121 --> 00:05:04,336
these are the ones I would consider turning

116
00:05:04,449 --> 00:05:06,263
on to get faster inference.
